<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Diyar Mudar</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Diyar Mudar is the medieval Arabic name for the western part of the Jazira, the upper Mesopotamian region along the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/euphrates">Euphrates</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balikh-river">Balikh</a>, named after the Mudar tribes settled there in early Islamic times. Its main towns included <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/raqqa">Raqqa</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harran">Harran</a>, Edessa and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saruj">Saruj</a>. In the 10th and 11th centuries it was contested by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hamdanid-dynasty">Hamdanids</a>, the Numayrid <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bedouin">Bedouin</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantines</a>.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Diyar Mudar, meaning abode of Mudar, was the westernmost of the three provinces of al-Jazira, or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upper-mesopotamia">Upper Mesopotamia</a>, the others being <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diyar-bakr">Diyar Bakr</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diyar-rabia">Diyar Rabi'a</a>. The medieval geographer al-Baladhuri held that all three were named after the main Arab tribes that settled there during the Muslim conquests of the 7th century, the Mudar in this case.</p><p>The province spread over both banks of the middle <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/euphrates">Euphrates</a>, from the area of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samosata">Samosata</a> to the town of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anah">Anah</a>, and took in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balikh-river">Balikh</a> valley and the lower reaches of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khabur-river">Khabur</a>. Raqqa in the south and Edessa, called al-Ruha in Arabic, in the north were its main cities; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harran">Harran</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saruj">Saruj</a>, now Suruç, were others.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In early Islamic times Diyar Mudar was usually part of al-Jazira. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hamdanid-dynasty">Hamdanids</a> took it in the mid-10th century, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sayf-al-dawla">Sayf al-Dawla</a> detached it from the Jazira and the Hamdanids of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mosul">Mosul</a> and placed it under his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emirate">Emirate</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aleppo">Aleppo</a>, while the revived <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantine Empire</a> attacked the region. When Hamdanid control ended, the Banu Numayr gained the upper hand: Waththab ibn Ja'bar al-Numayri was autonomous governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harran">Harran</a> by 1002, and the Byzantine general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/george-maniakes">George Maniakes</a> took Edessa in 1032.</p><p>From then on the north was mostly under Christian rule and subject to Armenian colonisation, while Arab nomadic tribes dominated the land from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harran">Harran</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/euphrates">Euphrates</a>. Seljuk raids in the 1060s and 1070s failed to take the region. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-manzikert">Battle of Manzikert</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philaretos-brachamios">Philaretos Brachamios</a> made Thoros governor of Edessa and Diyar Mudar. By 1086 the Seljuk sultan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malik-shah-i">Malik-Shah I</a> had united the province, but the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusade">Crusades</a> divided it again between the Christian County of Edessa in the north and a Muslim south until the mid-12th century. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ayyubid-empire">Ayyubids</a> won it under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saladin">Saladin</a> and held it until the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongol-invasion-of-the-levant">Mongol invasion of the Levant</a> in 1260.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Diyar Mudar” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Banu Numayr were a Bedouin tribe of the Amir ibn Sa'sa'a who moved into the <b>Diyar Mudar</b>, the region between the Euphrates and the Balikh.</blockquote><a href="/state/numayrids">Numayrids</a></li></ul></section>
